# Part Two — Today's Debates

> The examiner of 2024–2026 wants the topic applied to the present. These are the live debates of Topic 1. Each gives you facts, both sides and a line to take.

## Debate 1 · The transgender law and the courts

Covered fully in Chapter 11. The key facts: the Supreme Court recognised *khawaja siras* in ==2009==; the Act of ==2018== allowed self-perceived identity (section 3) and set inheritance by card gender (section 7); public controversy began in ==2022==; the Federal Shariat Court ruled against those sections on ==19 May 2023==; appeals were still pending before the Shariat Appellate Bench in 2026. The line to take: protect dignity and livelihood; let the courts and parliament settle the disputed question.

## Debate 2 · The world-wide backlash against "gender"

::: debate | Is "gender" an ideology?
Since the 2010s, movements in Europe, the Americas, Africa and Asia have attacked what they call "gender ideology" — the idea that gender is made by society — as a threat to family, religion and national culture. Some governments have stepped back from gender-equality promises or closed Gender Studies programmes. For example, Hungary removed Gender Studies from its list of approved master's programmes in ==2018==.
- **Critics of the field** say it undermines traditional values and biological reality.
- **Defenders** reply that Gender Studies is a research field like any other; that its findings on violence, health and work rest on evidence; and that closing departments attacks academic freedom.
-> Link it to Pakistan: similar charges of a "Western agenda" are made here (Chapter 5). The best defence of the field is careful, local, evidence-based research.
:::

## Debate 3 · "Masculinity in crisis" and hatred of women online

Young men in many countries — Pakistan included — face unemployment, high prices and changing expectations. Some online influencers sell these young men a return to a dominant, anti-women masculinity. At the same time, online abuse of women journalists, politicians and activists has grown. Masculinity studies (Chapter 9) explains the appeal: when the breadwinner role becomes hard to fulfil, the promise of control over women becomes attractive.
- **Researchers' answer:** do not simply condemn young men. Offer positive, "caring" models of manhood, economic opportunity, and education on respectful relationships.

## Debate 4 · Gender in textbooks

How school textbooks show women and men is a long-running debate in Pakistan. Studies have repeatedly found women shown mainly as mothers and housewives, and men as workers, soldiers and leaders. The **Single National Curriculum**, introduced from ==2021==, revived the argument over how girls and women appear in the books.
- **One side** wants textbooks to show the full range of women's roles — doctors, scientists, pilots, leaders — to widen girls' ambitions.
- **Another side** stresses cultural and religious values and resists what it sees as foreign influence on the curriculum.
-> The integration plan of Chapter 4 applies directly: gender-aware textbooks are the widest-reaching form of integration.

## Debate 5 · The future of Gender Studies departments

Gender Studies departments in Pakistan depend on public university budgets, which are under pressure, and on donor projects that come and go. Yet demand for gender expertise is growing — in government, aid agencies and private companies (for diversity, harassment committees and gender audits). The debate is whether the field can win steady funding and prove its worth through useful, local research.

## Debate 6 · Social media and gender rules

Social media has given Pakistani women new voices — businesswomen, teachers, comedians, activists — and new risks: harassment, blackmail and "honour"-based attacks on women who post online. The debate asks whether online platforms are loosening or tightening traditional femininities. Both are happening at once: platforms open space for new femininities while exposing women to new forms of control (Topic 7 covers online violence; Topic 5 the digital gender gap).

## Debate 7 · Does Gender Studies matter for Pakistan's development?

::: balance | Luxury or necessity?
Some say a poor country in economic crisis cannot afford "soft" subjects like Gender Studies. The evidence points the other way. Only ==24.4%== of Pakistan's working-age women are in the labour force (LFS 2024–25), so most of that half of the population is outside paid work. Closing even part of this gap would add greatly to national income, as many economic studies of South Asia have argued. The data, analysis and policy skills needed to close it — on transport, harassment, childcare, skills and attitudes — are exactly what Gender Studies produces.
-> Gender Studies is not a luxury for Pakistan; it is part of the toolkit of development (Topic 4) and of good government (Topic 6).
:::
